# ADR 0138: Domain-Neutral Graph Debug Shell and Runtime Debug Providers

Status: Proposed
Date: 2026-08-17
Amends: ADR 0123
Relates to: ADR 0006, ADR 0007, ADR 0008, ADR 0010, ADR 0013, ADR 0014, ADR 0033, ADR 0085, ADR 0103, ADR 0116

## Problem

Play mode currently gives Behavior Tree runtime debugging a dedicated Editor surface even when the running world has no Behavior Tree runner. That special-cases one graph domain and does not scale to Animation Graph, future Visual Script, or other executable graph domains.

ADR 0006-0013 intentionally keep the shared Graph foundation domain-neutral while compiled/runtime semantics remain domain-owned. ADR 0123 adds a useful Behavior Tree execution snapshot and read-only graph overlay, but its UI shape must not become the template copied once per graph domain.

Animation Preview also exists for authoring-time animation inspection. It is not a substitute for observing the actual Animation Controller, Animation Set resolution, transitions, parameters, events, and clip playback occurring in a live Play world.

The Editor needs one discoverable Graph Debug shell that reuses Graph foundation presentation without inventing a universal runtime graph semantic model.

## Decision

### 1. Play mode exposes one domain-neutral Graph Debug entry

The primary Play workspace is conceptually:

```text
Game View
Scene View
Graph Debug
```

`Graph Debug` represents runtime graph executions present in the current Play world. It is not a Behavior Tree, Animation, or Visual Script product name.

When no runtime graph execution is available, Graph Debug is normally hidden from the Play workspace selector so irrelevant tooling does not consume permanent space. If Graph Debug was already selected and the final observed graph disappears during the same Play session, the shell remains temporarily visible in an empty/disabled state explaining that no runtime graphs are currently available. This avoids unexpectedly throwing the user into another workspace while still removing the permanent empty tab on the next normal selection/render cycle.

The Editor MUST NOT show a fixed Behavior Tree-only Play tab merely because Behavior Tree support is compiled into the Editor.

### 2. Runtime graph discovery is provider-driven

The Graph Debug shell asks registered domain providers for currently debuggable runtime graph instances.

A conceptual instance descriptor contains:

- domain/provider identity;
- runtime Entity identity and display label when available;
- source graph stable identity;
- source asset reference/path resolved through normal project asset services;
- runtime generation/revision sufficient for stale-source detection;
- provider-owned instance key;
- short domain label such as `Animation Graph` or `Behavior Tree`.

The shell groups instances by runtime Entity and presents only instances that actually exist in the live world.

Example:

```text
Player
  Animation Graph: PlayerGraph

Enemy01
  Behavior Tree: EnemyAI

Enemy02
  Behavior Tree: EnemyAI
```

The instance list is transient Play state and is never serialized into authoring assets.

### 3. The shell owns domain-neutral presentation mechanics

The common Graph Debug shell owns only concerns that are valid for every graph domain:

- runtime graph instance selection;
- source graph resolution;
- read-only Graph Canvas;
- pan/zoom and Frame All;
- Node/Edge selection;
- stable `NodeId` / `EdgeId` source mapping;
- active/highlight primitives whose meaning is supplied by the provider;
- a details region;
- stale or missing source presentation;
- read-only debug state and Play-session lifetime;
- generic navigation to the authoring source graph.

The shell reuses the existing Graph/GraphView foundation. It does not create a second Graph document model.

### 4. Runtime semantics remain behind a domain-specific provider

Each executable graph domain supplies a debug provider/adapter. The provider converts its own runtime observations into a neutral presentation plus domain-specific details.

Conceptually:

```text
GraphDebugProvider
  discover(runtime) -> [GraphDebugInstance]
  snapshot(instance) -> GraphDebugSnapshot
  source(instance) -> graph identity/reference
  overlay(snapshot) -> neutral node/edge presentation
  details(snapshot, selection) -> provider-owned read-only details model
```

This is an architectural responsibility contract, not a promise that these exact Rust trait names or method signatures are public API.

The provider owns:

- what constitutes a runtime graph instance;
- runtime snapshot cadence and generation;
- domain-specific execution states;
- domain-specific transition/event history;
- semantic formatting of provider-specific details;
- mapping runtime compiled data back to stable source IDs.

The provider MUST NOT mutate the source graph from Graph Debug. Authoring actions navigate to the normal editor instead.

### 5. Behavior Tree becomes one provider

ADR 0123 remains authoritative for Behavior Tree runtime execution and snapshot semantics.

Its provider exposes at least:

- active path;
- Running node;
- latest Success / Failure;
- Abort / Reset reason;
- per-active-node elapsed time;
- Blackboard values;
- recent transitions;
- runtime/domain error.

The existing Behavior Tree graph-debug canvas and execution snapshot are migrated behind Graph Debug rather than copied into a second shell.

This ADR amends only the Editor presentation ownership implied by ADR 0123. It does not change Behavior Tree execution semantics or persisted graph data.

### 6. Animation Graph becomes a first-class provider

Animation Graph runtime debugging observes the real Play-mode Animation Controller and its resolved Animation Set.

The provider snapshot must make available, when applicable:

- runtime Entity;
- source Animation Graph;
- active Animation Set;
- current State;
- previous State and pending/next State where meaningful;
- active transition source and destination;
- transition/fade progress;
- state elapsed time and resolved clip playback time;
- graph parameter names, values, and trigger state;
- selected/current `MotionSlotId` and display name;
- Animation Set binding used for that slot;
- resolved `MotionSourceRef` / variant;
- final resolved Animation Clip identity;
- animation event history relevant to the observed execution;
- transition-selection reason sufficient to explain why a transition became eligible/taken;
- unresolved binding or runtime animation error.

Current State and transition endpoints map to source graph `NodeId`/`EdgeId` when the Animation Graph schema provides those stable identities. The canvas highlights the actual source nodes/edges rather than reconstructing a parallel animation-state diagram.

Animation Set resolution must be inspectable from the selected State/Motion Slot so a user can answer: “Which concrete clip is this state playing on this Entity, and why?”

### 7. Animation Preview and Graph Debug have separate purposes

Animation Preview remains an authoring-time tool.

Animation Preview is for deliberately trying clips, graph transitions, parameters, retargeting, and presentation before or without running the game. It may use preview-owned time and inputs.

Graph Debug is observation of the actual live Play controller. It must not silently substitute preview state, synthetic parameters, or an editor-owned playback clock for runtime state.

A user may navigate from Graph Debug to the Animation Graph/Animation Set editor or Animation Preview, but the surfaces do not share execution authority.

### 8. Source mismatch is explicit

A runtime instance observes a specific loaded/compiled generation. If the source working copy or persisted graph no longer matches the runtime generation, the shell reports `Source changed since runtime load` (or equivalent) and stops presenting source mapping as current.

The shell may continue showing provider-owned runtime facts that remain meaningful, but it MUST NOT highlight a node with the same display name as a substitute for the stable source identity.

Restart/reload of the relevant runtime execution establishes a new mapping.

### 9. The provider boundary is extensible, not a lowest-common-denominator runtime model

Future graph domains such as Visual Script may register providers without adding a new permanent Play tab.

Providers can supply domain-specific details and badges beyond the common active/highlight vocabulary. The common shell does not gain Behavior Tree statuses, animation fade fields, or Visual Script stack semantics as global enum variants merely to host them.

## Scope / Non-goals

In scope are Play-mode Graph Debug discovery, presentation architecture, provider responsibility, Behavior Tree migration, Animation Graph runtime-debug requirements, and the Animation Preview boundary.

This ADR does not implement Visual Script, change graph serialization, change Animation/Behavior execution algorithms, add authoring mutation to debug mode, or replace the existing Runtime Debugger for non-graph ECS/runtime inspection.

## UX model

Graph Debug has three coordinated regions:

1. runtime graph instance selector, grouped by Entity;
2. shared read-only Graph Canvas;
3. provider-specific details pane.

Selecting a Hierarchy/runtime entity may filter or prioritize instances for that Entity. Selecting an instance may update the runtime-entity selection when that mapping is unambiguous.

When several Entities execute the same source graph, each runtime instance remains independently selectable because execution state is per runtime Entity.

## Stable ID and serialization impact

No new persisted authoring IDs are introduced.

The shell relies on existing stable source identities: asset identity / `GraphId`, `NodeId`, `EdgeId`, `MotionSlotId`, and domain-defined runtime generation identities. Display labels are presentation only.

Debug snapshots are transient. Persisting them into project graph files is forbidden by this ADR.

## Public API and crate boundary impact

Graph document/canvas primitives remain in their existing Graph foundation / Editor presentation boundaries.

Runtime domains own their snapshot types. A small provider-facing DTO or trait may live at the Editor/runtime observation boundary, but `engine-authoring` MUST NOT depend on Behavior Tree or Animation runtime semantics.

`engine-editor` composes providers and owns the shell. Runtime Animation and Behavior crates expose read-only observations without depending on egui or Editor UI types.

No existing public authoring API, MCP tool, serialization format, or crate dependency direction is changed by the ADR itself.

## Migration / compatibility

The existing Behavior Tree debug snapshot remains valid. Its dedicated Editor workspace is migrated into the Behavior Tree provider.

Saved project content requires no migration. User preferences that identify the old Behavior Tree Play tab may be ignored or mapped to Graph Debug when a Behavior Tree instance exists.

## Testing strategy

Implementation must test:

- zero providers/instances: no permanent Graph Debug entry;
- one and several providers with deterministic Entity grouping;
- multiple runtime instances using the same source graph remain distinct;
- Behavior Tree snapshot parity before/after provider migration;
- Animation provider mappings for current State, transition, parameters, Motion Slot, Set binding, resolved motion/clip, and events;
- stale source generation prevents misleading source highlights;
- Node/Edge selection and Frame All remain domain-neutral;
- Graph Debug performs no authoring mutation;
- Animation Preview state cannot leak into live runtime snapshots;
- runtime entity disappearance produces safe empty-state behavior.

## Visual Validation requirements

Graph Debug implementation requires Visual Validation because correctness includes layout and live overlay readability.

Required captures include:

- Behavior Tree provider with active path and details;
- Animation Graph provider during a transition with source/destination and fade progress;
- instance selector containing Animation and Behavior domains;
- several Entities sharing one graph;
- stale-source state;
- graph disappearing while Graph Debug is selected;
- normal Play mode with no graph instances, proving no irrelevant permanent tab remains.

Text clipping, ambiguous active-state visualization, details covering the canvas, or a misleading stale-source highlight are failures.

## Rollout

1. Extract the current Behavior Tree canvas/debug presentation into the provider contract without semantic change.
2. Introduce Graph Debug shell and replace the dedicated Behavior Tree Play entry.
3. Add provider discovery and zero-instance visibility policy.
4. Add Animation Graph runtime snapshots and source-ID mapping.
5. Add Animation-specific details and Animation Set resolution inspection.
6. Add provider registration tests and documentation for future graph domains.

## Rejected alternatives

### Copy the Behavior Tree debugger for Animation Graph

Rejected. It duplicates source resolution, canvas state, selection, stale detection, and Play workspace UX and repeats the problem for every future domain.

### Move all runtime graph state into a universal Graph runtime type

Rejected. Graph foundation intentionally does not own domain execution semantics.

### Keep one fixed Play tab per graph domain

Rejected. It scales with installed features rather than the running world and permanently advertises irrelevant tools.

### Replace Animation Preview with live Graph Debug

Rejected. Authoring preview and observation of an actual runtime controller have different clocks, inputs, and purposes.

### Let Graph Debug edit the graph while observing it

Rejected for the first contract. Mixing runtime observation and authoring mutation complicates source-generation identity and stale-state semantics. Navigation to the normal editor is the safe boundary.
